{"id":"gen-1790966836-r58tn18wm4y5O3LHTqew","object":"chat.completion","created":1790966836,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            2,\n            3,\n            1,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a finite CSP with variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints c1..c10 are given, each specifying allowed pairs for a given ordered pair of variables. c1: (a,b) allowed: (3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2). c2: (b,c) allowed: (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0). c3: (c,d) allowed: (3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1). c4: (d,a) allowed: (2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3). c5: (e,f) allowed: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1). c6: (f,g) allowed: (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2). c7: (g,h) allowed: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0). c8: (h,e) allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). c9: (b,e) allowed: (1,2), (3,1). c10: (d,g) allowed: (2,1), (1,0).\n\nGoal: Enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right, i.e., variables e,f,g,h. The problem description says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to produce a list of tuples (e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (including those that involve left variables a,b,c,d). So we must find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP for all variables, then extract the assignments for e,f,g,h. Then output those tuples in lexicographic order (presumably ascending order for each tuple, comparing e then f then g then h). The envelope template has a placeholder tuple [0,0,0,0] which we need to replace with the full list.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" array updated accordingly.\n\nWe need to be careful: The envelope has fields \"read_refs\" and \"recipients\". We must keep them unchanged, except we need to replace the \"tuples\" in payload.claim.value.\n\nThe envelope template includes:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            0,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to replace the placeholder tuples with the full list.\n\nSo the main challenge is to solve the CSP.\n\nWe have 8 variables, domain size 4 each. There are 10 constraints. Could brute force manually? Let's attempt systematic reasoning.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h such that there exist assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, plus c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g), plus the others.\n\nBetter approach: solve the CSP systematically using backtracking. Let's try to enumerate all possible assignments for the left variables a,b,c,d, then see which e,f,g,h combos are possible.\n\nWe could try to reduce the search space using constraints.\n\nLet's start enumerating possible assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) involve e and g, which are right side variables. So we cannot fully decide left side without right side. However, we can treat e,f,g,h as variables to be solved simultaneously.\n\nThus we need to solve the whole CSP.\n\nWe can do a systematic search. There are 4^8 = 65536 possible assignments. That's not huge but manual enumeration is tedious. But we can prune heavily.\n\nLet's define constraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (0,2)\n\nc2: allowed (b,c):\n- (1,3)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,0)\n\nc3: allowed (c,d):\n- (3,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nc4: allowed (d,a):\n- (2,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,3)\n\nc5: allowed (e,f):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n\nc6: allowed (f,g):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n\nc7: allowed (g,h):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,0)\n\nc8: allowed (h,e):\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,0)\n\nc9: allowed (b,e):\n- (1,2)\n- (3,1)\n\nc10: allowed (d,g):\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n\nGoal: find all assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full solution.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions for all variables and collect distinct (e,f,g,h) combos.\n\nWe can approach by enumerating possibilities for right side variables first, then see if there exist left side assignments.\n\nGiven constraints c5-c8 define relationships among e,f,g,h. Let's analyze those.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)\n\nThus e,f,g,h must form a cycle: e->f via c5, f->g via c6, g->h via c7, h->e via c8. So we can treat this as a constraint satisfaction subgraph.\n\nWe can enumerate all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy these four constraints. Then for each, we need to check if there exist a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). So we need to find left side assignments consistent with the chosen e,f,g,h.\n\nThus first step: enumerate all (e,f,g,h) combos satisfying c5-c8.\n\nWe can brute force manually, but careful. Let's list possible values for e (0-3) and then possible f based on c5, then possible g based on f via c6, then possible h based on g via c7, then check h->e via c8.\n\nWe'll systematically generate.\n\nc5 allowed (e,f):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus possible e values: 0,1,2. (e cannot be 3 because no pair with e=3 in c5). So e ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nLet's enumerate each e:\n\nCase e=0:\n- f possibilities: from c5: (0,2) and (0,1). So f ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCase e=1:\n- f possibilities: (1,2) and (1,1). So f ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCase e=2:\n- f possibilities: (2,3). So f=3 only.\n\nThus we have limited combos.\n\nNow for each (e,f), we need to find g via c6.\n\nc6 allowed (f,g):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n\nThus for each f, possible g values:\n\n- f=3: allowed g: from c6, (3,1) and (3,2). So g ∈ {1,2}.\n- f=2: allowed g: (2,1) and (0,2). So g ∈ {1,0}.\n- f=1: allowed g: (1,0). So g=0 only.\n\nThus we can enumerate.\n\nNow for each (e,f,g), we need h via c7.\n\nc7 allowed (g,h):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,0)\n\nThus for each g, possible h:\n\n- g=0: none? Let's check: c7 includes (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0). So g=0 does not appear as first element. So if g=0, no allowed h. So any assignment with g=0 is invalid.\n\nThus g cannot be 0. So we must restrict to g values that appear in c7: g ∈ {1,2,3}. Indeed c7 includes g=1,2,3.\n\nThus from c6, we need g ∈ {1,2,3}. So we must eliminate f=2 case where g could be 0 (since g=0 is not allowed). So for f=2, only g=1 is allowed (since g ∈ {1,0}, but g=0 invalid). So for f=2, g must be 1.\n\nSimilarly, for f=3, g ∈ {1,2}. Both allowed.\n\nFor f=1, g=0 only, but g=0 invalid, so f=1 yields no solution. So any assignment with f=1 is impossible.\n\nThus we can prune.\n\nNow enumerate possible combos:\n\nCase e=0:\n- f=2: then g must be 1 (since g=0 invalid). So (e,f,g) = (0,2,1). Then h from c7: for g=1, allowed h: (1,0) only (since c7 includes (1,0)). So h=0. Then check c8 (h,e): (0,0) allowed? c8 allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). So (h=0, e=0) is allowed (0,0). So this combo (0,2,1,0) is valid.\n\n- f=1: f=1 leads to g=0 invalid, so discard.\n\nThus for e=0, only one valid tuple: (e,f,g,h) = (0,2,1,0).\n\nCase e=1:\n- f=2: g ∈ {1,0}, but g=0 invalid, so g=1. So (1,2,1). Then h from c7: g=1 => h=0 (only). So h=0. Check c8: (h=0, e=1) => (0,1) allowed? c8 allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). (0,1) not allowed. So this fails.\n\n- f=1: f=1 leads to g=0 invalid, so discard.\n\nThus e=1 yields no valid combos.\n\nCase e=2:\n- f=3: g ∈ {1,2}. So two possibilities:\n\n-- g=1: then h from c7: g=1 => h=0. So (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0). Check c8: (h=0, e=2) => (0,2) allowed? c8 includes (0,2). Yes, allowed. So (2,3,1,0) is valid.\n\n-- g=2: then h from c7: g=2 => allowed h: (2,3), (2,2), (2,0). So h ∈ {3,2,0}. So we have three possibilities:\n\n--- h=3: (2,3,2,3). Check c8: (h=3, e=2) => (3,2) allowed? c8 allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). (3,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n--- h=2: (2,3,2,2). c8: (2,2) allowed? No. So invalid.\n\n--- h=0: (2,3,2,0). c8: (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is allowed. So (2,3,2,0) is valid.\n\nThus for e=2, we have valid combos: (2,3,1,0) and (2,3,2,0). Also maybe (2,3,1,0) we already have.\n\nThus total valid (e,f,g,h) combos from constraints c5-c8 are:\n\n1. (0,2,1,0)\n2. (2,3,1,0)\n3. (2,3,2,0)\n\nWe need to check if there are any other combos we missed. Let's double-check all possibilities.\n\nWe enumerated e ∈ {0,1,2}. For e=0, f ∈ {2,1}. f=2 gave g=1, h=0, valid. f=1 gave g=0 invalid. So only (0,2,1,0).\n\nFor e=1, f ∈ {2,1}. f=2 gave g=1, h=0, but c8 fails. f=1 gave g=0 invalid. So none.\n\nFor e=2, f=3 only. g ∈ {1,2}. For g=1, h=0, valid. For g=2, h ∈ {3,2,0}, only h=0 valid. So (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nThus only three combos.\n\nNow for each of these combos, we need to check if there exist assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). Also note that c9 involves b and e; c10 involves d and g.\n\nThus for each (e,f,g,h) we need to find a,b,c,d such that:\n\n- (a,b) ∈ c1 allowed pairs.\n- (b,c) ∈ c2 allowed pairs.\n- (c,d) ∈ c3 allowed pairs.\n- (d,a) ∈ c4 allowed pairs.\n- (b,e) ∈ c9 allowed pairs.\n- (d,g) ∈ c10 allowed pairs.\n\nThus we need to solve a small CSP for a,b,c,d given e,g (and also b must satisfy c9 with e, and d must satisfy c10 with g). So we need to find all possible a,b,c,d combos for each (e,g) pair.\n\nThus we need to consider each of the three combos:\n\n1. (e=0, f=2, g=1, h=0)\n2. (e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0)\n3. (e=2, f=3, g=2, h=0)\n\nBut note that f does not appear in left constraints; it's irrelevant for left side. So only e and g matter for left side constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve for each (e,g) pair:\n\n- (e=0,g=1)\n- (e=2,g=1)\n- (e=2,g=2)\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d such that:\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ A1 = {(3,1),(2,0),(1,3),(0,1),(0,2)}.\nc2: (b,c) ∈ A2 = {(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(0,2),(0,0)}.\nc3: (c,d) ∈ A3 = {(3,2),(1,3),(3,1),(2,0),(2,1)}.\nc4: (d,a) ∈ A4 = {(2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3)}.\nc9: (b,e) ∈ A9 = {(1,2),(3,1)}. So b must be such that (b,e) is allowed. For each e, we need possible b.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So d must be such that (d,g) allowed.\n\nThus we need to find all solutions (a,b,c,d) for each (e,g). Then we can collect the (e,f,g,h) combos that have at least one solution. The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically.\" That means we need to output the (e,f,g,h) tuples that are part of any full solution. So we need to include only those combos for which there exists at least one full assignment for left variables. If a combo has no left solution, we must exclude it.\n\nThus we need to check each of the three combos for existence of left assignments.\n\nLet's analyze each case.\n\n### Case 1: e=0, g=1\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d satisfying constraints with e=0, g=1.\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed pairs: (1,2) and (3,1). Since e=0, (b,0) must be in A9. But A9 only has (1,2) and (3,1). So there is no pair with e=0. Therefore, there is no possible b such that (b,0) ∈ A9. Therefore, case (e=0,g=1) has no solution. So the tuple (0,2,1,0) is invalid overall. So we must exclude it.\n\nThus only remaining combos are those with e=2.\n\n### Case 2: e=2, g=1\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d with e=2, g=1.\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ A9 => (b,2) must be allowed. A9 pairs: (1,2) and (3,1). So (b,2) is allowed only for b=1 (since (1,2) is there). (3,1) has e=1, not relevant. So b must be 1.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs. With b=1, we need (a,1) ∈ A1. A1 = {(3,1),(2,0),(1,3),(0,1),(0,2)}. Which pairs have second component 1? (3,1) and (0,1). So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs. b=1, so need (1,c) ∈ A2. A2 = {(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(0,2),(0,0)}. Which pairs have first component 1? Only (1,3). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs. c=3, need (3,d) ∈ A3. A3 = {(3,2),(1,3),(3,1),(2,0),(2,1)}. Which pairs have first component 3? (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs. Must be in A4 = {(2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3)}.\n\nWe have a ∈ {3,0} and d ∈ {2,1}. Let's enumerate possibilities:\n\n- If a=3, d=2: check (d,a) = (2,3) which is in A4 (yes, (2,3) present). So (a=3,d=2) works.\n\n- If a=3, d=1: (1,3) check A4: does (1,3) exist? A4 includes (2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3). (1,3) not present. So invalid.\n\n- If a=0, d=2: (2,0) check A4: (2,0) not present. A4 has (2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3). So (2,0) invalid.\n\n- If a=0, d=1: (1,0) is present (yes). So (a=0,d=1) works.\n\nThus we have two possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) in this case:\n\n1. a=3, b=1, c=3, d=2.\n2. a=0, b=1, c=3, d=1.\n\nNow also need to check c10: (d,g) ∈ A10, with g=1. A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So (d,g) must be either (2,1) or (1,0). For each candidate:\n\n- For (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2): d=2, g=1 => (2,1) is allowed. Good.\n\n- For (a=0,b=1,c=3,d=1): d=1, g=1 => (1,1) is not in A10 (only (2,1) or (1,0)). So this fails c10. So only the first assignment works.\n\nThus for case (e=2,g=1), there is at least one solution: (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2). So the tuple (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0) is valid.\n\nThus we keep (2,3,1,0).\n\n### Case 3: e=2, g=2\n\nNow e=2, g=2.\n\nc9: (b,e) => (b,2) must be in A9. As before, only (1,2) works. So b=1.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nc1: (a,b) => (a,1) ∈ A1. As before, a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nc2: (b,c) => (1,c) ∈ A2 => c=3 (only (1,3)). So c=3.\n\nc3: (c,d) => (3,d) ∈ A3 => d ∈ {2,1} as before.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ A4. Need to check possibilities.\n\nc10: (d,g) => (d,2) must be in A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So (d,2) must be either (2,1) or (1,0). But both have second component 1 or 0, not 2. So there is no pair with second component 2. Thus (d,g) cannot be satisfied for g=2. Therefore, no solution for e=2,g=2. So the tuple (2,3,2,0) is invalid.\n\nThus only one valid tuple remains: (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0).\n\nBut we need to verify if there are any other possible (e,f,g,h) combos that we might have missed due to oversight. Let's double-check enumeration of (e,f,g,h) satisfying c5-c8.\n\nWe enumerated e ∈ {0,1,2}. Let's double-check each e thoroughly.\n\n**e=0**:\n\nc5: (0,f) allowed: (0,2) and (0,1). So f=2 or 1.\n\n- f=2: c6: (2,g) allowed: (2,1) and (0,2). So g=1 or 0. g=0 invalid (c7). So g=1. c7: (1,h) allowed: (1,0). So h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is allowed. So (0,2,1,0) is valid.\n\n- f=1: c6: (1,g) allowed: (1,0). So g=0. g=0 invalid (c7). So no solution.\n\nThus e=0 yields only (0,2,1,0).\n\n**e=1**:\n\nc5: (1,f) allowed: (1,2) and (1,1). So f=2 or 1.\n\n- f=2: c6: (2,g) allowed: (2,1) and (0,2). So g=1 or 0. g=0 invalid. So g=1. c7: (1,h) => h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,1) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (0,2),(0,3),(3,0),(1,1),(0,0). (0,1) not allowed. So invalid.\n\n- f=1: c6: (1,g) => g=0. g=0 invalid. So no solution.\n\nThus e=1 yields none.\n\n**e=2**:\n\nc5: (2,f) allowed: (2,3). So f=3.\n\nc6: (3,g) allowed: (3,1) and (3,2). So g=1 or 2.\n\n- g=1: c7: (1,h) => h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is allowed. So (2,3,1,0) valid.\n\n- g=2: c7: (2,h) allowed: (2,3),(2,2),(2,0). So h ∈ {3,2,0}. Check each:\n\n-- h=3: c8: (3,2) allowed? c8 includes (0,2),(0,3),(3,0),(1,1),(0,0). (3,2) not allowed. So invalid.\n\n-- h=2: c8: (2,2) allowed? No.\n\n-- h=0: c8: (0,2) allowed? Yes. So (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nThus e=2 yields two combos: (2,3,1,0) and (2,3,2,0).\n\nThus enumeration seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check if there are any other constraints that involve e,f,g,h beyond c5-c8? No, only those.\n\nThus only three combos satisfy c5-c8. Among them, only (2,3,1,0) yields a full solution. So the answer list for component right should be just that one tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify if there might be other solutions for left variables that could allow other (e,f,g,h) combos beyond those three, perhaps because we might have missed some combos due to constraints c5-c8 being not the only constraints for right side? Actually c5-c8 are the only constraints that involve e,f,g,h. So any solution must satisfy those. So the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos are those three. So if (0,2,1,0) and (2,3,2,0) are invalid due to left side constraints, then the only valid tuple is (2,3,1,0). So answer should be a JSON envelope with \"tuples\": [[2,3,1,0]].\n\nWe need to ensure lexicographic ordering. Since only one tuple, it's fine.\n\nNow we must ensure that the envelope matches the template exactly, with fields unchanged except \"tuples\". The template includes \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with [ [2,3,1,0] ].\n\nCheck other fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload.claim.claim_id\", \"dependencies\", \"provenance\", \"revision\", \"state_revision\", \"status\", \"value.component\", \"value.variables\", etc. All should stay same. Also \"read_refs\" includes a reference to \"claim:right-tuples\" with revision 0, state_revision 0. That seems fine.\n\nWe need to output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }. No markdown code fences.\n\nThus final answer:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            2,\n            3,\n            1,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that there are no trailing commas. Also ensure that the JSON is valid.\n\nDouble-check the envelope: The \"payload.claim\" includes \"dependencies\" array with one object. That seems correct.\n\nNow we need to double-check that the \"tuples\" field is an array of arrays of integers. Yes.\n\nNow confirm that we haven't missed any other possible (e,f,g,h) combos due to possible alternative assignments for left side variables that could allow other e,f,g,h combos. Since c5-c8 constraints are independent of left side, the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos are those three. So no need to consider others.\n\nThus answer is as above.\n\nBut we need to double-check the constraints for left side to ensure there are no other solutions for (e=0,g=1) that could be possible via other b values? Wait c9: (b,e) allowed pairs: (1,2) and (3,1). For e=0, there is no allowed b. So indeed no solution for e=0. So (0,2,1,0) is out.\n\nNow check (e=2,g=2) case: c10: (d,g) allowed pairs: (2,1) and (1,0). For g=2, no allowed d. So indeed no solution.\n\nThus only (2,3,1,0) works.\n\nNow we must ensure that the left side solution we found indeed satisfies all constraints c1-c4 and c9, c10. Let's verify:\n\nSolution: a=3, b=1, c=3, d=2, e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0.\n\nCheck constraints:\n\nc1 (a,b): (3,1) allowed? Yes, (3,1) is in c1.\n\nc2 (b,c): (1,3) allowed? Yes, (1,3) is in c2.\n\nc3 (c,d): (3,2) allowed? Yes, (3,2) is in c3.\n\nc4 (d,a): (2,3) allowed? Yes, (2,3) is in c4.\n\nc5 (e,f): (2,3) allowed? Yes, (2,3) is in c5.\n\nc6 (f,g): (3,1) allowed? Yes, (3,1) is in c6.\n\nc7 (g,h): (1,0) allowed? Yes, (1,0) is in c7.\n\nc8 (h,e): (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c8.\n\nc9 (b,e): (1,2) allowed? Yes, (1,2) is in c9.\n\nc10 (d,g): (2,1) allowed? Yes, (2,1) is in c10.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the tuple is correct.\n\nNow we need to output the JSON envelope exactly as required.\n\nOne nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": { ... } }. The \"value\" field includes \"component\": \"right\", \"tuples\": [ ... ], \"variables\": [ \"e\", \"f\", \"g\", \"h\" ]. That's correct.\n\nNow ensure that we didn't inadvertently include any extra spaces or line breaks that might be considered formatting issues? The problem says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we should output the JSON without any extra commentary. It's okay to have pretty formatting (indentation) as long as it's valid JSON. Usually they accept pretty printed JSON. The template is pretty printed with indentation. So we can output the same indentation style.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON as above.\n\nBut before finalizing, let's double-check the envelope fields: The \"payload.claim\" includes \"dependencies\": [ { \"claim_revision\": 1, \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\" } ]. That matches the template.\n\nThe \"read_refs\" includes two entries: one for \"problem:csp-5103\" with revision 1, state_revision 1; another for \"claim:right-tuples\" with revision 0, state_revision 0. That matches.\n\nNow ensure that the \"message_id\" is \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\". Yes.\n\nNow ensure that the \"problem_id\" is \"csp-5103\". Yes.\n\nNow ensure that the \"sender\" is \"right-proposer\". Yes.\n\nNow ensure that the \"recipients\" list is [\"left-reviewer\",\"shared-integrator\"].\n\nNow ensure that the \"reply_to\" is null.\n\nNow ensure that the \"schema_version\" is 1.\n\nNow ensure that the \"value\" includes \"component\": \"right\". Yes.\n\nNow ensure that the \"variables\" list is [\"e\",\"f\",\"g\",\"h\"].\n\nNow ensure that the \"tuples\" list contains exactly one tuple [2,3,1,0] (as integers). Yes.\n\nThus final answer is correct.\n\nBut before finalizing, let's double-check that there aren't any other possible (e,f,g,h) combos that we might have missed because we assumed c5-c8 constraints are independent of left side. However, there is also c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) which involve e and g respectively, but those are already considered when checking left side solutions. So the only possible combos for e and g that can be extended to left side solutions are those where there exists b and d satisfying c9 and c10. So we need to check if there are any combos where e=0,g=1 that could be extended with some b,d? But c9 prohibits any b for e=0. So no.\n\nSimilarly, e=2,g=2 is prohibited by c10. So only e=2,g=1 works. So indeed only one tuple.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to output exactly that JSON.\n\nOne more thing: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": { ... } }. The \"payload\" field is inside the envelope. The envelope also has \"payload\": { \"claim\": { ... } }. Yes.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the JSON is syntactically correct. Let's produce the JSON with proper quoting and commas.\n\nI'll write the JSON exactly as above.\n\nOne more nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": { ... } }. The \"claim\" includes \"dependencies\": [ { \"claim_revision\": 1, \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\" } ]. That's correct.\n\nNow check for any missing commas: After \"dependencies\": [ { \"claim_revision\": 1, \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\" } ], there is a comma before \"provenance\". Yes.\n\nNow check that \"payload.claim.value\" includes \"component\": \"right\", \"tuples\": [ [2,3,1,0] ], \"variables\": [ \"e\", \"f\", \"g\", \"h\" ].\n\nNow check that \"payload.claim\" includes \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1, \"status\": \"active\". Yes.\n\nNow check that the envelope includes \"read_refs\": [ { \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1 }, { \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\", \"revision\": 0, \"state_revision\": 0 } ].\n\nNow check that \"recipients\": [ \"left-reviewer\", \"shared-integrator\" ].\n\nNow check that \"reply_to\": null.\n\nNow check that \"schema_version\": 1.\n\nNow check that \"sender\": \"right-proposer\".\n\nNow check that the envelope includes \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\".\n\nNow check that the envelope includes \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\".\n\nNow check that the envelope includes \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\".\n\nNow check that the envelope includes \"payload\": { \"claim\": { ... } }.\n\nThus all good.\n\nNow we should output the JSON.\n\nOne more nuance: The problem statement says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output the JSON exactly as described, with no extra commentary. So we will output the JSON.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON above.\n\nNow I will produce the final answer.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a finite CSP with variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints c1..c10 are given, each specifying allowed pairs for a given ordered pair of variables. c1: (a,b) allowed: (3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2). c2: (b,c) allowed: (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0). c3: (c,d) allowed: (3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1). c4: (d,a) allowed: (2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3). c5: (e,f) allowed: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1). c6: (f,g) allowed: (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2). c7: (g,h) allowed: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0). c8: (h,e) allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). c9: (b,e) allowed: (1,2), (3,1). c10: (d,g) allowed: (2,1), (1,0).\n\nGoal: Enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right, i.e., variables e,f,g,h. The problem description says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically.\" So we need to produce a list of tuples (e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (including those that involve left variables a,b,c,d). So we must find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP for all variables, then extract the assignments for e,f,g,h. Then output those tuples in lexicographic order (presumably ascending order for each tuple, comparing e then f then g then h). The envelope template has a placeholder tuple [0,0,0,0] which we need to replace with the full list.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" array updated accordingly.\n\nWe need to be careful: The envelope has fields \"read_refs\" and \"recipients\". We must keep them unchanged, except we need to replace the \"tuples\" in payload.claim.value.\n\nThe envelope template includes:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            0,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to replace the placeholder tuples with the full list.\n\nSo the main challenge is to solve the CSP.\n\nWe have 8 variables, domain size 4 each. There are 10 constraints. Could brute force manually? Let's attempt systematic reasoning.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h such that there exist assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, plus c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g), plus the others.\n\nBetter approach: solve the CSP systematically using backtracking. Let's try to enumerate all possible assignments for the left variables a,b,c,d, then see which e,f,g,h combos are possible.\n\nWe could try to reduce the search space using constraints.\n\nLet's start enumerating possible assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) involve e and g, which are right side variables. So we cannot fully decide left side without right side. However, we can treat e,f,g,h as variables to be solved simultaneously.\n\nThus we need to solve the whole CSP.\n\nWe can do a systematic search. There are 4^8 = 65536 possible assignments. That's not huge but manual enumeration is tedious. But we can prune heavily.\n\nLet's define constraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (0,2)\n\nc2: allowed (b,c):\n- (1,3)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,0)\n\nc3: allowed (c,d):\n- (3,2)\n- (1,3)\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nc4: allowed (d,a):\n- (2,3)\n- (1,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,3)\n\nc5: allowed (e,f):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n\nc6: allowed (f,g):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n\nc7: allowed (g,h):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,0)\n\nc8: allowed (h,e):\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,0)\n\nc9: allowed (b,e):\n- (1,2)\n- (3,1)\n\nc10: allowed (d,g):\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n\nGoal: find all assignments for e,f,g,h that can be extended to full solution.\n\nWe need to enumerate all solutions for all variables and collect distinct (e,f,g,h) combos.\n\nWe can approach by enumerating possibilities for right side variables first, then see if there exist left side assignments.\n\nGiven constraints c5-c8 define relationships among e,f,g,h. Let's analyze those.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)\n\nThus e,f,g,h must form a cycle: e->f via c5, f->g via c6, g->h via c7, h->e via c8. So we can treat this as a constraint satisfaction subgraph.\n\nWe can enumerate all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy these four constraints. Then for each, we need to check if there exist a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). So we need to find left side assignments consistent with the chosen e,f,g,h.\n\nThus first step: enumerate all (e,f,g,h) combos satisfying c5-c8.\n\nWe can brute force manually, but careful. Let's list possible values for e (0-3) and then possible f based on c5, then possible g based on f via c6, then possible h based on g via c7, then check h->e via c8.\n\nWe'll systematically generate.\n\nc5 allowed (e,f):\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus possible e values: 0,1,2. (e cannot be 3 because no pair with e=3 in c5). So e ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nLet's enumerate each e:\n\nCase e=0:\n- f possibilities: from c5: (0,2) and (0,1). So f ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCase e=1:\n- f possibilities: (1,2) and (1,1). So f ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCase e=2:\n- f possibilities: (2,3). So f=3 only.\n\nThus we have limited combos.\n\nNow for each (e,f), we need to find g via c6.\n\nc6 allowed (f,g):\n- (3,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n\nThus for each f, possible g values:\n\n- f=3: allowed g: from c6, (3,1) and (3,2). So g ∈ {1,2}.\n- f=2: allowed g: (2,1) and (0,2). So g ∈ {1,0}.\n- f=1: allowed g: (1,0). So g=0 only.\n\nThus we can enumerate.\n\nNow for each (e,f,g), we need h via c7.\n\nc7 allowed (g,h):\n- (1,0)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n- (2,0)\n\nThus for each g, possible h:\n\n- g=0: none? Let's check: c7 includes (1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0). So g=0 does not appear as first element. So if g=0, no allowed h. So any assignment with g=0 is invalid.\n\nThus g cannot be 0. So we must restrict to g values that appear in c7: g ∈ {1,2,3}. Indeed c7 includes g=1,2,3.\n\nThus from c6, we need g ∈ {1,2,3}. So we must eliminate f=2 case where g could be 0 (since g=0 is not allowed). So for f=2, only g=1 is allowed (since g ∈ {1,0}, but g=0 invalid). So for f=2, g must be 1.\n\nSimilarly, for f=3, g ∈ {1,2}. Both allowed.\n\nFor f=1, g=0 only, but g=0 invalid, so f=1 yields no solution. So any assignment with f=1 is impossible.\n\nThus we can prune.\n\nNow enumerate possible combos:\n\nCase e=0:\n- f=2: then g must be 1 (since g=0 invalid). So (e,f,g) = (0,2,1). Then h from c7: for g=1, allowed h: (1,0) only (since c7 includes (1,0)). So h=0. Then check c8 (h,e): (0,0) allowed? c8 allowed (h,e) pairs: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). So (h=0, e=0) is allowed (0,0). So this combo (0,2,1,0) is valid.\n\n- f=1: f=1 leads to g=0 invalid, so discard.\n\nThus for e=0, only one valid tuple: (e,f,g,h) = (0,2,1,0).\n\nCase e=1:\n- f=2: g ∈ {1,0}, but g=0 invalid, so g=1. So (1,2,1). Then h from c7: g=1 => h=0 (only). So h=0. Check c8: (h=0, e=1) => (0,1) allowed? c8 allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). (0,1) not allowed. So this fails.\n\n- f=1: f=1 leads to g=0 invalid, so discard.\n\nThus e=1 yields no valid combos.\n\nCase e=2:\n- f=3: g ∈ {1,2}. So two possibilities:\n\n-- g=1: then h from c7: g=1 => h=0. So (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0). Check c8: (h=0, e=2) => (0,2) allowed? c8 includes (0,2). Yes, allowed. So (2,3,1,0) is valid.\n\n-- g=2: then h from c7: g=2 => allowed h: (2,3), (2,2), (2,0). So h ∈ {3,2,0}. So we have three possibilities:\n\n--- h=3: (2,3,2,3). Check c8: (h=3, e=2) => (3,2) allowed? c8 allowed: (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). (3,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n--- h=2: (2,3,2,2). c8: (2,2) allowed? No. So invalid.\n\n--- h=0: (2,3,2,0). c8: (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is allowed. So (2,3,2,0) is valid.\n\nThus for e=2, we have valid combos: (2,3,1,0) and (2,3,2,0). Also maybe (2,3,1,0) we already have.\n\nThus total valid (e,f,g,h) combos from constraints c5-c8 are:\n\n1. (0,2,1,0)\n2. (2,3,1,0)\n3. (2,3,2,0)\n\nWe need to check if there are any other combos we missed. Let's double-check all possibilities.\n\nWe enumerated e ∈ {0,1,2}. For e=0, f ∈ {2,1}. f=2 gave g=1, h=0, valid. f=1 gave g=0 invalid. So only (0,2,1,0).\n\nFor e=1, f ∈ {2,1}. f=2 gave g=1, h=0, but c8 fails. f=1 gave g=0 invalid. So none.\n\nFor e=2, f=3 only. g ∈ {1,2}. For g=1, h=0, valid. For g=2, h ∈ {3,2,0}, only h=0 valid. So (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nThus only three combos.\n\nNow for each of these combos, we need to check if there exist assignments for a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1-c4, c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). Also note that c9 involves b and e; c10 involves d and g.\n\nThus for each (e,f,g,h) we need to find a,b,c,d such that:\n\n- (a,b) ∈ c1 allowed pairs.\n- (b,c) ∈ c2 allowed pairs.\n- (c,d) ∈ c3 allowed pairs.\n- (d,a) ∈ c4 allowed pairs.\n- (b,e) ∈ c9 allowed pairs.\n- (d,g) ∈ c10 allowed pairs.\n\nThus we need to solve a small CSP for a,b,c,d given e,g (and also b must satisfy c9 with e, and d must satisfy c10 with g). So we need to find all possible a,b,c,d combos for each (e,g) pair.\n\nThus we need to consider each of the three combos:\n\n1. (e=0, f=2, g=1, h=0)\n2. (e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0)\n3. (e=2, f=3, g=2, h=0)\n\nBut note that f does not appear in left constraints; it's irrelevant for left side. So only e and g matter for left side constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve for each (e,g) pair:\n\n- (e=0,g=1)\n- (e=2,g=1)\n- (e=2,g=2)\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d such that:\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ A1 = {(3,1),(2,0),(1,3),(0,1),(0,2)}.\nc2: (b,c) ∈ A2 = {(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(0,2),(0,0)}.\nc3: (c,d) ∈ A3 = {(3,2),(1,3),(3,1),(2,0),(2,1)}.\nc4: (d,a) ∈ A4 = {(2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3)}.\nc9: (b,e) ∈ A9 = {(1,2),(3,1)}. So b must be such that (b,e) is allowed. For each e, we need possible b.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So d must be such that (d,g) allowed.\n\nThus we need to find all solutions (a,b,c,d) for each (e,g). Then we can collect the (e,f,g,h) combos that have at least one solution. The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically.\" That means we need to output the (e,f,g,h) tuples that are part of any full solution. So we need to include only those combos for which there exists at least one full assignment for left variables. If a combo has no left solution, we must exclude it.\n\nThus we need to check each of the three combos for existence of left assignments.\n\nLet's analyze each case.\n\n### Case 1: e=0, g=1\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d satisfying constraints with e=0, g=1.\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed pairs: (1,2) and (3,1). Since e=0, (b,0) must be in A9. But A9 only has (1,2) and (3,1). So there is no pair with e=0. Therefore, there is no possible b such that (b,0) ∈ A9. Therefore, case (e=0,g=1) has no solution. So the tuple (0,2,1,0) is invalid overall. So we must exclude it.\n\nThus only remaining combos are those with e=2.\n\n### Case 2: e=2, g=1\n\nWe need to find a,b,c,d with e=2, g=1.\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ A9 => (b,2) must be allowed. A9 pairs: (1,2) and (3,1). So (b,2) is allowed only for b=1 (since (1,2) is there). (3,1) has e=1, not relevant. So b must be 1.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs. With b=1, we need (a,1) ∈ A1. A1 = {(3,1),(2,0),(1,3),(0,1),(0,2)}. Which pairs have second component 1? (3,1) and (0,1). So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs. b=1, so need (1,c) ∈ A2. A2 = {(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(0,2),(0,0)}. Which pairs have first component 1? Only (1,3). So c=3.\n\nThus c=3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs. c=3, need (3,d) ∈ A3. A3 = {(3,2),(1,3),(3,1),(2,0),(2,1)}. Which pairs have first component 3? (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs. Must be in A4 = {(2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3)}.\n\nWe have a ∈ {3,0} and d ∈ {2,1}. Let's enumerate possibilities:\n\n- If a=3, d=2: check (d,a) = (2,3) which is in A4 (yes, (2,3) present). So (a=3,d=2) works.\n\n- If a=3, d=1: (1,3) check A4: does (1,3) exist? A4 includes (2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3). (1,3) not present. So invalid.\n\n- If a=0, d=2: (2,0) check A4: (2,0) not present. A4 has (2,3),(1,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,3). So (2,0) invalid.\n\n- If a=0, d=1: (1,0) is present (yes). So (a=0,d=1) works.\n\nThus we have two possible assignments for (a,b,c,d) in this case:\n\n1. a=3, b=1, c=3, d=2.\n2. a=0, b=1, c=3, d=1.\n\nNow also need to check c10: (d,g) ∈ A10, with g=1. A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So (d,g) must be either (2,1) or (1,0). For each candidate:\n\n- For (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2): d=2, g=1 => (2,1) is allowed. Good.\n\n- For (a=0,b=1,c=3,d=1): d=1, g=1 => (1,1) is not in A10 (only (2,1) or (1,0)). So this fails c10. So only the first assignment works.\n\nThus for case (e=2,g=1), there is at least one solution: (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2). So the tuple (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0) is valid.\n\nThus we keep (2,3,1,0).\n\n### Case 3: e=2, g=2\n\nNow e=2, g=2.\n\nc9: (b,e) => (b,2) must be in A9. As before, only (1,2) works. So b=1.\n\nThus b=1.\n\nc1: (a,b) => (a,1) ∈ A1. As before, a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nc2: (b,c) => (1,c) ∈ A2 => c=3 (only (1,3)). So c=3.\n\nc3: (c,d) => (3,d) ∈ A3 => d ∈ {2,1} as before.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ A4. Need to check possibilities.\n\nc10: (d,g) => (d,2) must be in A10 = {(2,1),(1,0)}. So (d,2) must be either (2,1) or (1,0). But both have second component 1 or 0, not 2. So there is no pair with second component 2. Thus (d,g) cannot be satisfied for g=2. Therefore, no solution for e=2,g=2. So the tuple (2,3,2,0) is invalid.\n\nThus only one valid tuple remains: (e,f,g,h) = (2,3,1,0).\n\nBut we need to verify if there are any other possible (e,f,g,h) combos that we might have missed due to oversight. Let's double-check enumeration of (e,f,g,h) satisfying c5-c8.\n\nWe enumerated e ∈ {0,1,2}. Let's double-check each e thoroughly.\n\n**e=0**:\n\nc5: (0,f) allowed: (0,2) and (0,1). So f=2 or 1.\n\n- f=2: c6: (2,g) allowed: (2,1) and (0,2). So g=1 or 0. g=0 invalid (c7). So g=1. c7: (1,h) allowed: (1,0). So h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is allowed. So (0,2,1,0) is valid.\n\n- f=1: c6: (1,g) allowed: (1,0). So g=0. g=0 invalid (c7). So no solution.\n\nThus e=0 yields only (0,2,1,0).\n\n**e=1**:\n\nc5: (1,f) allowed: (1,2) and (1,1). So f=2 or 1.\n\n- f=2: c6: (2,g) allowed: (2,1) and (0,2). So g=1 or 0. g=0 invalid. So g=1. c7: (1,h) => h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,1) allowed? c8 allowed pairs: (0,2),(0,3),(3,0),(1,1),(0,0). (0,1) not allowed. So invalid.\n\n- f=1: c6: (1,g) => g=0. g=0 invalid. So no solution.\n\nThus e=1 yields none.\n\n**e=2**:\n\nc5: (2,f) allowed: (2,3). So f=3.\n\nc6: (3,g) allowed: (3,1) and (3,2). So g=1 or 2.\n\n- g=1: c7: (1,h) => h=0. c8: (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is allowed. So (2,3,1,0) valid.\n\n- g=2: c7: (2,h) allowed: (2,3),(2,2),(2,0). So h ∈ {3,2,0}. Check each:\n\n-- h=3: c8: (3,2) allowed? c8 includes (0,2),(0,3),(3,0),(1,1),(0,0). (3,2) not allowed. So invalid.\n\n-- h=2: c8: (2,2) allowed? No.\n\n-- h=0: c8: (0,2) allowed? Yes. So (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nThus e=2 yields two combos: (2,3,1,0) and (2,3,2,0).\n\nThus enumeration seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check if there are any other constraints that involve e,f,g,h beyond c5-c8? No, only those.\n\nThus only three combos satisfy c5-c8. Among them, only (2,3,1,0) yields a full solution. So the answer list for component right should be just that one tuple.\n\nBut we need to verify if there might be other solutions for left variables that could allow other (e,f,g,h) combos beyond those three, perhaps because we might have missed some combos due to constraints c5-c8 being not the only constraints for right side? Actually c5-c8 are the only constraints that involve e,f,g,h. So any solution must satisfy those. So the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos are those three. So if (0,2,1,0) and (2,3,2,0) are invalid due to left side constraints, then the only valid tuple is (2,3,1,0). So answer should be a JSON envelope with \"tuples\": [[2,3,1,0]].\n\nWe need to ensure lexicographic ordering. Since only one tuple, it's fine.\n\nNow we must ensure that the envelope matches the template exactly, with fields unchanged except \"tuples\". The template includes \"tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ]. We need to replace that with [ [2,3,1,0] ].\n\nCheck other fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload.claim.claim_id\", \"dependencies\", \"provenance\", \"revision\", \"state_revision\", \"status\", \"value.component\", \"value.variables\", etc. All should stay same. Also \"read_refs\" includes a reference to \"claim:right-tuples\" with revision 0, state_revision 0. That seems fine.\n\nWe need to output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }. No markdown code fences.\n\nThus final answer:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            2,\n            3,\n            1,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that there are no trailing commas. Also ensure that the JSON is valid.\n\nDouble-check the envelope: The \"payload.claim\" includes \"dependencies\" array with one object. That seems correct.\n\nNow we need to double-check that the \"tuples\" field is an array of arrays of integers. Yes.\n\nNow confirm that we haven't missed any other possible (e,f,g,h) combos due to possible alternative assignments for left side variables that could allow other e,f,g,h combos. Since c5-c8 constraints are independent of left side, the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos are those three. So no need to consider others.\n\nThus answer is as above.\n\nBut we need to double-check the constraints for left side to ensure there are no other solutions for (e=0,g=1) that could be possible via 